    "# 02 — Signal Processing, Windowing & Feature Extraction\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook builds the two dataset representations used by the rest of the project:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Windowed raw signal** (`windows_X.npy` / `windows_y.npy` / `windows_subject.npy`) — fed\n",
    "   directly to the deep learning models (1D CNN, LSTM).\n",
    "2. **Hand-crafted feature table** (`features.csv`) — fed to the classical ML models\n",
    "   (XGBoost, LightGBM), which need a fixed-length feature vector rather than a raw sequence.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Pipeline: Raw Accelerometer Data → Signal Processing → Windowing → Feature Extraction → ML\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why windowing?\n",
    "A single 50Hz reading (a single instant in time) carries almost no information about what\n",
    "activity is happening — you cannot tell \"walking\" from \"standing\" by looking at one sample.\n",
    "Activities are recognizable from the *pattern* of the signal over a short time span, so we\n",
    "group consecutive readings into fixed-size **windows** and treat each window as one ML sample.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Design choices made here (documented explicitly, since they affect every result downstream):\n",
    "- **Window size: 150 samples = 3 seconds** at 50Hz. Long enough to capture at least one full\n",
    "  gait cycle for walking/running, short enough to stay a reasonably \"pure\" single-activity\n",
    "  segment.\n",
    "- **50% overlap** between consecutive windows — a standard HAR practice that both increases the\n",
    "  number of training samples and avoids losing information at window boundaries.\n",
    "- **Windows are built per-subject** — a window never mixes signal from two different people.\n",
    "- **Majority-vote labeling with a 90% purity threshold** — a window is only kept if at least 90%\n",
    "  of its raw samples share the same activity label. Windows straddling an activity transition\n",
    "  (e.g. sitting → standing) are discarded rather than mislabeled.\n"
   ]
