    "## 2. The headline finding: classical ML won this comparison\n",
    "\n",
    "On this dataset, with a proper subject-wise train/test split, **XGBoost clearly outperformed\n",
    "both the 1D CNN and the LSTM** on macro F1 — the metric that matters most given how imbalanced\n",
    "the activity classes are. This runs against the popular assumption that \"deep learning beats\n",
    "classical ML\" — and the reasons why are worth taking seriously rather than glossing over,\n",
    "because they're the actual insight of this project.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why the deep learning models likely underperformed here\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Not enough subjects for the models to learn subject-invariant patterns.** With only 22\n",
    "   people total (17 for training after holding out 5 for test), a CNN/LSTM learning directly\n",
    "   from raw signal has relatively few distinct \"movement signatures\" to generalize across. It's\n",
    "   plausible the models partly learned *person-specific* gait/sensor-placement quirks from the\n",
    "   17 training subjects rather than fully person-invariant activity patterns — quirks that don't\n",
    "   transfer to the 5 test subjects. Classical HAR literature generally agrees: raw-signal deep\n",
    "   learning tends to need either many more subjects, data augmentation, or self-supervised\n",
    "   pretraining to close this gap.\n",
    "2. **The validation-vs-test gap confirms this.** During training, validation accuracy (measured\n",
    "   on held-out *windows*, but from the *same* subjects seen in training) reached 98%+ for the\n",
    "   CNN — yet test accuracy on truly new subjects was under 89%. That ~10-point drop is a real\n",
    "   generalization gap, and it's a textbook illustration of exactly why subject-wise splitting\n",
    "   matters (see notebook 02) — a random split would have hidden this gap entirely and reported a\n",
    "   misleadingly high number.\n",
    "3. **Hand-crafted features are a strong prior.** The 80 features going into XGBoost (means,\n",
    "   standard deviations, dominant frequency, spectral energy, cross-axis correlation...) encode\n",
    "   substantial domain knowledge about what separates human activities, effectively giving the\n",
    "   classical models a head start that the deep learning models had to try to discover from raw\n",
    "   signal alone, with less data to do it.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Where deep learning would likely close the gap (a genuine open question this project doesn't\n",
    "### settle)\n",
    "- More subjects (HARTH itself is a fairly modest dataset by deep learning standards)\n",
    "- Data augmentation (jittering, rotation, time-warping on the raw signal)\n",
    "- A pretrained/self-supervised backbone rather than training from scratch\n",
    "- More extensive hyperparameter tuning and a larger training budget (this project trained on a\n",
    "  single CPU core, capping how much architecture search and epoch budget was practical)\n"
   ]
  },
